    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days, so misuse before that window is not ruled out. The burst itself is also not yet confirmed as misuse.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The burst (new fingerprint, residential proxy, payout change) happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes, so the evidence already existed and was not flagged. This assumes the check could see request and account-change records, which the case does not state.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is consistent with one owner, but also with one fraudster redirecting several accounts or a shared payment service.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** An owner whose payouts were diverted would probably have complained within 30 days, so the silence leans slightly toward legitimacy. It is weak because reports can lag or never be filed.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new origin and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with payout changes, is reason enough to investigate.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** On the recorded figures D has 0 of 100 against 2 of 100 for each of the others. With counts this small the gap may not mean much.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined 5 percent is an average, so one account can sit above it while another sits below.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. It only covers accounts held under that person's identity.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence, because they may have looked at the same evidence or the second may have read the first note.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** A blocked payment cannot produce a chargeback, so the absence of one says nothing; it was blocked as suspected fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared templates, descriptor pattern and settlement accounts are enough to justify looking at the four together, though not enough to conclude they are one operation.\n\nRESULT MF-010 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Yes | A3: No, not by itself | A4: Yes, weakly | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No | B3: Yes | B4: Cannot tell | B5: No | B6: Yes"
